---
/**
 * /about/ . THE ONLY PAGE ON THIS DOMAIN THAT CLAIMS THE PERSON.
 *
 * ===========================================================================
 * WHY THIS PAGE IS A BIOGRAPHY AND NOT A COLOPHON
 * ===========================================================================
 *
 * Every article title on this site leads with its SUBJECT and never with a
 * person's name. That is correct: it is what a brand-first publication should do
 * and it is the safest position available in an estate where two domains fronting
 * one name already cost khaledhawari.ca page one in August 2026.
 *
 * The consequence is that no article here will ever rank for his name, because
 * a piece about Ethereum in 2016 is not an answer to a question about a person.
 * By design. Which leaves exactly one page on this domain able to carry the
 * string "Khaled Hawari" into a search result, and this is it. So it is written
 * as a page worth reading on its own: what the site is, why the name means what
 * it means, the standard the record is held to, and who is writing it and why
 * that is not an odd pairing.
 *
 * ===========================================================================
 * THE RULES THIS PAGE FOLLOWS, ALL OF THEM MECHANICAL
 * ===========================================================================
 *
 * THE H1 LEADS WITH THE NAME. The <title> does not. That split is deliberate and
 * it is the same split kna-group.com arrived at after a sweep found its founder
 * page opening with the same "Khaled Hawari," as two pages on .ca: the title is
 * the contested slot across domains, the H1 is not, and the H1 is the stronger
 * of the two for the person anyway. So the title leads with this site's own
 * framing and carries the name after the divider, and the H1 opens with the name
 * beside the occupation.
 *
 * THE NAME IS ALWAYS ONE CONTIGUOUS STRING. Never "Khaled (Kal) Hawari", which
 * contains neither "Khaled Hawari" nor "Kal Hawari" as a phrase anyone can
 * search for. The nickname is introduced in its own sentence and declared in the
 * Person node's alias list, which is the one place that rendering belongs.
 * scripts/verify-subject.mjs fails the build on the parenthesised form anywhere
 * in visible copy or head metadata.
 *
 * THE FINGERPRINT ATTRIBUTES SIT NEXT TO THE NAME. Ottawa, Ontario. Tax and
 * financial consultant. Founder of Kodelytics Inc. and KNA Group. Commerce at
 * the University of Ottawa and Carleton University. English, French and Arabic.
 * Search engines do not disambiguate by name, they disambiguate by the set of
 * attributes that reliably co-occur with a name across many independent
 * documents, and those are the attributes the other four domains carry too.
 *
 * NOTHING ON THIS PAGE REFERENCES ANY OTHER PERSON, ANY OUTLET OR ANY MATTER,
 * and no sentence here distinguishes him from anybody. Publishing a denial
 * creates the association in our own words, on a domain we control, and indexes
 * it permanently. There is no safe wording for one. The rule is positive only,
 * and scripts/verify-subject.mjs enforces it.
 *
 * IT CARRIES ProfilePage AND ITS `mainEntity` IS THE CANONICAL PERSON, and it is
 * the only route in the build allowed to. See src/lib/schema.ts. The person's
 * own `mainEntityOfPage` still points at khaledhawari.ca, because "this page is
 * about him" and "the primary document about him is over there" are both true at
 * once, and only the second is a claim this domain has no business making.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Datum from "../components/Datum.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import portrait from "../assets/khaled-hawari.jpg";
import { site, ESTATE, PERSON_SAME_AS } from "../lib/site";
import { routeMeta } from "../lib/seo";

const trail = [{ label: "About", href: "/about/" }];
const linkedin = PERSON_SAME_AS.find((url) => url.includes("linkedin.com"))!;
---

<Base {...routeMeta.about} breadcrumbs={trail}>
  <Breadcrumbs trail={trail} />

  <header class="page-head">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">
        <span class="eyebrow__num">00</span>
        <span>About ISM50 and its author</span>
      </p>
      {/* THE NAME LEADS THE H1 AND THE OCCUPATION SITS BESIDE IT. One
          contiguous string, followed immediately by the discriminating
          attribute, which is the arrangement that carries the most signal in the
          strongest heading slot on the page. */}
      <h1 style="max-width: 22ch;">Khaled Hawari writes a history of crypto.</h1>
      <p class="page-head__lede" style="max-width: 60ch;">
        He is a tax and financial consultant in Ottawa, Ontario, and the founder
        of Kodelytics Inc. and KNA Group. This page explains what ISM50 is, why
        it is called that, and why the pairing is less odd than it sounds.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section--tight">
    <div class="container-narrow">
      <Datum label="The name" />

      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Fifty is the line, and the name is the argument</h2>
        <p>
          The Institute for Supply Management publishes a diffusion index. It is
          not a price and not a growth rate. It is a count of how many
          respondents said a thing improved rather than deteriorated, and fifty
          is the point at which those two groups are exactly the same size. Above
          fifty the thing is expanding. Below fifty it is contracting. The number
          says nothing at all about magnitude, only about direction, and that is
          precisely what makes it useful.
        </p>
        <p>
          Crypto has spent fifteen years crossing that line. Not once, and not
          all at the same time: capital, attention, credit, belief, users,
          developers and regulation each crossed it more than once, in both
          directions, and rarely in step with each other or with the price. A
          period that looks in hindsight like an obvious peak was, to the people
          inside it, a period in which several of those series were still rising
          and one had quietly turned.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is the whole editorial position. Every piece on this site should be
          able to answer three questions: what was expanding, what was
          contracting, and who could tell at the time. The third one is usually
          where the piece actually is, because the honest answer is normally that
          almost nobody could, including several people whose later accounts say
          otherwise.
        </p>
        <p>
          The number is also, by now, close to a joke in this particular corner of
          the internet. That is fine. A name that works on two levels is a better
          name than one that works on one, and the site is not going to explain
          the pun again anywhere else.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section--tight">
    <div class="container-narrow">
      <Datum label="The standard" />

      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Crypto history is badly documented, and that is the opportunity</h2>
        <p>
          Try to check almost anything from before 2015 and you will run into the
          same three problems. Primary sources have quietly disappeared: a press
          release that half the academic literature cites now returns a 404, and
          the only surviving copy of its text is a block quote in a paper about
          something else. Narratives have been rewritten by their participants,
          usually in a direction that improves them. And the confident numbers
          everyone repeats often trace back to nothing: a figure that appears in
          a peer-reviewed paper with no citation, then in a hundred articles
          citing the paper.
        </p>
        <p>
          So the rules here are strict, and they are enforced by the build rather
          than by good intentions.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Nothing is invented.</strong> No date, no figure, no quote and
          no claim about what a named person said or did unless it can be
          checked. Where a number cannot be sourced it is cut rather than
          softened, because a hedged invented number is still an invented number.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Where a claim is contested, the contest is named.</strong> Not
          "some say", which is a way of avoiding the work. The actual parties,
          what each of them concluded, and the documents.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Where a source is dead, the piece says so and says what it
          said.</strong> A dead link is itself a fact about the record, and
          quietly dropping it hides the thing that most needed pointing out.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>There are no prices and no forecasts.</strong> This site quotes
          no market capitalisation, states no view on what anything is worth, and
          makes no prediction. Everything here is written with hindsight the
          people involved did not have, which is a reason for humility rather
          than a licence to extend the line. See
          <a href="/disclaimer/">what this site is not</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section--tight">
    <div class="container-narrow">
      <Datum label="The author" />

      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Why a tax and financial consultant writes this</h2>

        <p style="margin-bottom: var(--space-5);">
          <Image
            src={portrait}
            alt="Khaled Hawari"
            width={160}
            height={160}
            style="width: 96px; height: 96px; object-fit: cover; filter: grayscale(1) contrast(1.05);"
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </p>

        <p>
          Khaled Hawari is a tax and financial consultant in Ottawa, Ontario. He
          founded Kodelytics Inc., an Ontario corporation doing technical delivery
          work for agencies, and KNA Group, which runs the finance function for
          incorporated Canadian companies. He studied commerce at the University
          of Ottawa and at Carleton University, and works in English, French and
          Arabic.
        </p>
        {/* THE NICKNAME GETS ITS OWN SENTENCE. The parenthesised form contains
            neither name as a searchable phrase, so it is never used; both full
            strings appear here contiguously instead, and the alias list in the
            Person node declares the rest. */}
        <p>
          He goes by Kal. Kal Hawari and Khaled Hawari are the same person, and
          in Ottawa both forms turn up on the client side of an engagement, which is the
          only reason the distinction is worth a sentence at all.
        </p>
        <p>
          The route into this subject was not enthusiasm. It was that clients
          started arriving with exchange exports nobody could reconcile, and
          reconciling them turned out to require knowing what a given platform
          had actually been doing in a given year: which of its internal transfers
          were real, what its withdrawal halt in some particular month had meant,
          whether a token had been distributed or sold, and what the contract had
          done as opposed to what the announcement had promised. Answering those
          questions properly means reading primary sources, and reading primary
          sources for long enough turns into knowing the history.
        </p>
        <p>
          It also means having repeatedly found the received account to be wrong.
          That is the specific thing this site is for. A great deal of what
          everybody knows about crypto history is a press release that was never
          checked, and the correction is usually sitting in a court filing, a
          mailing list archive or a contract that has been public the whole time.
        </p>
        <p>
          The financial work is on
          <a href="https://khaledhawari.ca/about-khaled-hawari/">khaledhawari.ca</a>,
          and it stays there. Nothing on ISM50 is guidance for a reader's own
          situation, and this site does not write about what anybody owes anyone.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p style="margin-top: var(--space-6);">
        <a href={linkedin} class="mono">LinkedIn</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section--tight">
    <div class="container-narrow">
      <Datum label="Elsewhere" />

      <div class="prose">
        <h2>The other four</h2>
        <p>
          Four sites, four subjects, one person. They do not overlap on purpose:
          the split is what keeps each of them the answer to its own question
          rather than two of them competing for one.
        </p>
      </div>

      <ul class="estate-list" style="margin-top: var(--space-5);">
        {
          ESTATE.map((entry) => (
            <li>
              <a href={entry.href} class="estate-list__host">
                {entry.label}
              </a>
              <p class="estate-list__note">{entry.note}</p>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
